('discovery','gate_latch','Explain how a simple garden gate latch works.','A latch holds a gate closed by resting against a fixed catch. The moving piece is lifted before the gate can swing. Its position must allow the gate to close without scraping the ground. The worker checks the hinge side before moving the catch because a sagging gate can make a sound latch appear badly placed. Screws are tightened after the parts are aligned. The latch is tried from both sides before the job is considered complete. A little space around the moving piece prevents ordinary dirt from immediately blocking it.'),
('discovery','chalk_line','Describe marking a straight line with a chalk line.','The worker measures two points and secures the chalk line at the first point. The string is drawn tightly toward the second point without dragging across the surface. When its position is correct, the worker lifts the middle slightly and releases it. The string leaves a fine trace of chalk where it strikes. The marks are checked before they are used as a guide. Dust and rough surfaces can make the trace uneven. The string is rewound after use so that it does not become tangled or carry moisture into its case.'),
('discovery','wooden_spoon','Describe finishing a wooden spoon.','The maker checks the bowl of the spoon for rough patches and then smooths the handle. Sanding follows the grain rather than cutting deep scratches across it. The rim is rounded enough to feel comfortable without becoming too thin. Dust is removed before an appropriate food contact finish is applied. The maker checks the care requirements of that finish. The spoon is allowed to dry fully before use. Later cleaning avoids leaving it soaking for a long period. A small inspection after drying reveals whether another gentle smoothing pass is needed.'),
('discovery','wheelbarrow','Explain balancing a load in a wheelbarrow.','A wheelbarrow carries part of its load over a wheel while the person supports the handles. Placing heavy material close to the wheel can reduce the force needed at the handles. The load must still remain stable when the barrow turns or crosses a small bump. Uneven material is arranged so it does not slide toward one side. The person checks the route before lifting. A full barrow is moved slowly enough to correct small changes in balance. At the destination, the contents are tipped carefully rather than released with an uncontrolled push.'),
('confirmation','desk_tray','Describe organizing small papers in a desk tray.','The worker separates current notes from papers that are no longer needed. A small tray keeps active papers together without hiding the labels on their edges. Related sheets are grouped before they are placed in the tray. A heavy stack is divided so that a page can be removed without lifting everything above it. The worker leaves room for new material instead of filling the tray completely. Dates are checked again when the contents are reviewed. A clear arrangement helps another person find the same sheet without depending on memory of its original position.'),
('confirmation','fishing_float','Explain how a fishing float stays visible.','A float rests partly above the surface because it displaces enough water to support its weight and the attached load. Its shape makes a small movement easier to see. Too much added weight can pull it below the surface even when nothing else changes. The user adjusts the arrangement before relying on its position. Waves and wind can also move the float. Observing it for a moment helps distinguish ordinary motion from a different pull. After use, the line and float are checked for damage before they are stored.'),
('confirmation','shoe_brush','Describe cleaning a pair of muddy shoes.','The owner lets loose mud become firm enough to remove without spreading it further. A brush clears the tread and the edges of the sole. The upper material is cleaned according to its care instructions rather than treated with the same method as every other shoe. Small stones are removed from the grooves. Laces can be taken out when they prevent the tongue from drying. The shoes are left in moving air away from excessive heat. Before wearing them again, the owner checks that the inside is dry and that the soles remain secure.'),
('confirmation','pot_lid','Explain how a lid changes cooking in a pot.','A lid slows the escape of warm moist air from a pot. Some steam condenses on its cooler underside and returns as drops. Keeping that moisture inside can change how quickly the contents lose water. A lid is not a substitute for checking the heat. Vigorous boiling can still push liquid toward the edge. The cook lifts the lid so that escaping steam moves away from hands. A suitable place is prepared for the hot lid before it is set down. The contents are checked before the pot is covered again.'),
('confirmation','basket_weave','Describe inspecting a woven basket.','The worker turns the basket slowly and checks the rim before looking at the base. Broken strands can leave a sharp edge or allow nearby strands to move out of place. The handle is tested with a gentle pull rather than a sudden heavy load. Dirt is brushed from the gaps so the weave remains visible. A loose section is noted for repair before the basket is filled. Drying is gradual because strong heat may change the material. A sound basket distributes the weight through connected strips rather than through one narrow strand.'),
('confirmation','shelf_bracket','Explain supporting a small shelf with brackets.','A shelf rests on brackets fixed securely to a wall or another support. The brackets are spaced so that the board does not bend excessively between them. The worker checks both the fixing points and the condition of the wall. The shelf is placed level before its final position is secured. Heavy objects are arranged within the load that the fittings can support. A loose bracket is repaired before more weight is added. Measuring carefully at the start avoids trying to correct a tilted shelf by stacking objects under one corner.'),
('confirmation','lemon_press','Describe using a hand press for a lemon.','The cook places a cut piece of lemon in a clean press and positions a bowl below it. The handles are brought together gradually so that juice flows into the bowl. The opening is kept clear of hands. Seeds and larger pieces can be collected with a small strainer if the recipe requires it. The empty peel is removed before another piece is inserted. The press is rinsed soon after use so that dried pulp does not collect in its joints. It is left open while drying and stored only after its moving parts are clean.'),
('confirmation','coat_hook','Explain attaching a coat hook to a board.','The worker marks the position of the hook and checks that there is enough room around it for a coat. The fitting is held in place while the fixing points are marked. Suitable holes are prepared without splitting the wood. The screws are tightened evenly so the hook sits flat. The worker tests it with a gentle pull before hanging an ordinary load. A board with a crack near the fixing needs attention rather than a larger pile of clothing. The final position is checked to make sure the hook does not obstruct a nearby door.'),
